mathedu,mathedu:ad08d221a61db1f6,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Treats total cost as cost per liter,ok,assume it costs $x$ cents. ... \(\frac{1}{2}: 50 =\frac{1}{4}: x\),ok,a spirit and water solution is sold in a market . the cost per liter of the solution is directly proportional to the part ( fraction ) of spirit ( by volume ) the solution has . a solution of 1 liter of spirit and 1 liter of water costs 50 cents . how many cents does a solution of 1 liter of spirit and 3 liters of water cost ?,assume it costs $x$ cents. $$ \begin{aligned} \frac{1}{1+1}: 50 & =\frac{1}{1+3}: x \\ \frac{1}{2}: 50 & =\frac{1}{4}: x \\ x & =25 \end{aligned} $$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#178
mathedu,mathedu:9dfe24a1889e7117,,Comprehension error,Converts a mixed number to an incorrect improper fraction,ok,car speed $=50 \times \frac{13}{5}=130 \mathrm{km} / \mathrm{hr}$,ok,the average speed of a car is 1 3 / 5 times the avg speed of a bike . a tractor covers 575 km in 23 hrs . how much distance will the car cover in 4 hrs if the speed of the bike is twice speed of the tractor ?,tractor speed is $575 / 23=25 \mathrm{~km} / \mathrm{hr}$ bike speed $=25 \times 2=50 \mathrm{~km} / \mathrm{hr}$ car speed $=50 \times \frac{13}{5}=130 \mathrm{km} / \mathrm{hr}$ $130 \times 4=520 \mathrm{~km}$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#189
mathedu,mathedu:75416622d5ed0863,,Comprehension error,Misidentifies the variables in a stated pair sum,ok,a+b=200 1 (2),ok,"a , b and c have rs . 250 between them , a and c together have rs . 200 and b and c rs . 350 . how much does c have ?",\begin{aligned} & \left\{\begin{array}{l} a+b+c=250 \ldots \text { (1) } \\ a+b=200 \ldots \text { (2) } \\ b+c=350 \ldots \text { (3) } \end{array}\right. \\ & \text { (1)- (2) } \Rightarrow c=50  \end{aligned},dataset/time_series_split/train.json#191
mathedu,mathedu:5a7ad9d09aa7cd56,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Confuses lost revenue with loss relative to total cost,ok,the loss percent $ =\frac{1.1 x y-0.88 x y}{x y}$,ok,a fair price shopkeeper takes 10 % profit on his goods . he lost 20 % goods during theft . his loss percent is :,"Assume the cost per goods is $\chi \&$ the number of goods is $y$ $$ \text { original revenue }=1.1 x y $$ revenue after 10 ss $20 \%$ goods $=1.1 X \cdot 0,8 y$ $$ =0.88 x y $$ $$ \begin{aligned} \text { the loss percent } & =\frac{1.1 x y-0.88 x y}{x y} \\ & =\frac{0.22 x y}{x y}=0.22 \\ & =22 \%  \end{aligned} $$",dataset/time_series_split/train.json#193
