mathedu,mathedu:761438a9d0e728b2,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Averages speeds directly instead of accounting for travel times,ok,\( \left.\frac{63+\frac{1}{2} \cdot 63}{2}=\frac{63+31.5}{2}=\frac{94.5}{2}=47.25 \text { (miles} / \mathrm{hr} )  \),ok,"a car traveled from san diego to san francisco at an average speed of 63 miles per hour . if the journey back took twice as long , what was the average speed of the trip ?",$$ \left.\frac{63+\frac{1}{2} \cdot 63}{2}=\frac{63+31.5}{2}=\frac{94.5}{2}=47.25 \text { (miles} / \mathrm{hr} )  $$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#631
mathedu,mathedu:21eadbd508064e3b,,Careless error,Counts a nonexistent conjugate expression among the available choices,ok,\Rightarrow(x+y)(x-y) \&(x+5 y)(x-5 y),ok,"if two of the 4 expressions x + y , x + 5 y , x - y , 5 x + y are chosen at random , what is the probability that their product will be of the form of x ^ 2 - ( by ) ^ 2 , where b is an integer ?","$$ \left\{\begin{array}{l} x+y \\ x+5 y \\ x-y \\ 5 x+y \end{array}\right. $$ $\because b$ is integer $\therefore$ the multiple of $y$ should be the same, $$ \because x^2-(b y)^2=(x+\sqrt{b} y)(x-\sqrt{b} y) $$ $\therefore$ the two expression should a plus one and a minus one$$ \begin{aligned} & \Rightarrow(x+y)(x-y) \&(x+5 y)(x-5 y) \\ \frac{2}{C_2^4} \\ & =\frac{1}{3} \end{aligned} $$",dataset/time_series_split/train.json#635
mathedu,mathedu:99a466224338b701,,Unfinished answer,Stops after finding one exclusive count without computing the requested difference,ok,So the numbers of students who only read book b is $500-500 \times 25 \%=375$,ok,"of 1000 surveyed students , 20 % of those who read book a also read book b and 25 % of those who read book b also read book a . if each student read at least one of the books , what is the difference between the number of students who read only book a and the number of students who read only book b ?",Assure $x$ student read book a $y$ student read book b $$ \begin{aligned} & 20 \% x=25 \% y \\ & 4 x=5 y \\ & x=\frac{5}{4} y \\ & y+\frac{5}{4} y-\frac{1}{4} y=1000 \\ & 2 y=1000 \\ & y=500 \end{aligned} $$ So the numbers of students who only read book b is $500-500 \times 25 \%=375$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#638
mathedu,mathedu:f75e47e39cd8e9d3,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,,label_conflict,\frac{5}{3} \div 5=\frac{1}{3},ok,"the hiker walking at a constant rate of 5 miles per hour is passed by a cyclist traveling in the same direction along the same path at 20 miles per hour . the cyclist stops to wait for the hiker 5 minutes after passing her , while the hiker continues to walk at her constant rate , how many minutes must the cyclist wait until the hiker catches up ?",\begin{aligned} & 20 \times \frac{5}{60}=\frac{5}{3} \\ & \frac{5}{3} \div 5=\frac{1}{3} \\ & 60 \times \frac{1}{3}=20 \mathrm{~min} \end{aligned},dataset/time_series_split/train.json#641
mathedu,mathedu:021402cc6e3ceb36,,Comprehension error,Reverses the order of quantities in a stated ratio,ok,c d & =2 \times 6= 12,ok,"a , b , c , d and e are 5 consecutive points on a straight line . if bc = 2 cd , de = 5 , ab = 5 and ac = 11 , what is the length of ae ?",\begin{aligned} b c & =a c-a b \\ & =11-5=6 \\ c d & =2 \times 6= 12 \\ a e & =a c+c d+d e \\ & =11+12+5 \\ & =28 \end{aligned},dataset/time_series_split/train.json#650
mathedu,mathedu:276e1bc07d9c06b5,,Algebraic error,Makes an arithmetic error when dividing by a decimal,ok,0.3 x=2.25 \\ & x=67.5,ok,"a manufacturer produces a certain men ' s athletic shoe in integer sizes from 8 to 17 . for this particular shoe , each unit increase in size corresponds to a 1 / 4 - inch increase in the length of the shoe . if the largest size of this shoe is 30 % longer than the smallest size , how long , in inches , is the shoe in size 15 ?",$17-8=9$ Assume the size 8 shoes is $x$ inch $$ \begin{aligned} & x+9 \times \frac{1}{4}=(1+30 \%) x \\ & x+2.25=1.3 x \\ & 0.3 x=2.25 \\ & x=67.5 \\ & 67.5+(15-8) \times \frac{1}{4}=69.25 \end{aligned} $$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#651
