mathedu,mathedu:b8270491c929cbfc,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Applies the loss and gain percentages to the rate in the wrong direction,ok,\frac{1600}{101} \times \frac{144}{100},ok,if a man lost 1 % by selling oranges at the rate of 16 a rupee at how many a rupee must he sell them to gain 44 % ?,\begin{aligned} & \frac{16 \cdot x}{x}=0.01 \\ & x=1600-100 x \\ 101 x & =1600 \\ x & =\frac{1600}{101} \\ \frac{1600}{101} \times \frac{144}{100} & \\ & =\frac{2304}{101} \\ & =22.81 \end{aligned},dataset/time_series_split/train.json#733
mathedu,mathedu:51d78174aae75593,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Uses total capital instead of total capital–time contributions as the sharing denominator,ok,3960 \times \frac{12000}{39000} \times \frac{9}{12},ok,"suresh started a business , investing rs . 18000 . after 3 months and 4 months respectively , rohan and sudhir joined him with capitals of 12000 and 9000 . at the end of the year the total profit was rs . 3960 . what is the difference between rohan ’ s and sudhir ’ s share in the profit ?",$18000+12000+9000=39000 \Rightarrow$ total capital $12-3=9 \Rightarrow$ the time rohan have joined until the end of the year. $12-4=8 \Rightarrow$ the time sudhir have joined until the end of the year. the rohan's share in the profit $$ \Rightarrow 3960 \times \frac{12000}{39000} \times \frac{9}{12}=396 \times \frac{1000}{3900} \times 9=66 \times \frac{9000}{650} = 913.85 $$ the sudhir's share in the profit $$ \begin{aligned} & \Rightarrow 3960 \times \frac{9000}{39000} \times \frac{8}{12}=1300 \times \frac{18000}{39000}=1320 \times \frac{6}{13} = 609.23 \\ & 913.85-609.23=304.62 \end{aligned} $$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#737
mathedu,mathedu:69d3c407e79113b8,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Adds individual production times instead of adding production rates,ok,z+b =\frac{3}{25} \text { ( hour per widget) },ok,"machine z takes 2 more hours than machine b to make 20 widgets . if working together , the machines can make 25 widgets in 3 hours , how long will it take machine z to make 40 widgets ?",\begin{aligned} & \left\{\begin{aligned} z+b & =\frac{3}{25} \text { ( hour per widget) } \\ z-b & =\frac{2}{20} \\ 2 z & =\frac{3}{25}+\frac{3}{20} \\ & =\frac{12+15}{100}=\frac{27}{100} \\ z & =\frac{27}{200} \\ \frac{27}{200} \times 40 & =\frac{27}{5} \text { hours } \end{aligned}\right. \end{aligned},dataset/time_series_split/train.json#739
