mathedu,mathedu:86fa1d27b5322a19,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Counts symmetric integer pairs without restricting them to primes,ok,"20 \rightarrow 1 \& 39,2 \& 38,3 \& 37 ; \cdots 17 \& 23",ok,"two different primes may be said torhymearound an integer if they are the same distance from the integer on the number line . for instance , 3 and 7 rhyme around 5 . what integer w between 1 and 20 , inclusive , has the greatest number of distinct rhyming primes around it ?","$$ \begin{aligned} & \because \quad 20 \rightarrow 1 \& 39,2 \& 38,3 \& 37 ; \cdots 17 \& 23, \\ & 18 \& 22,19 \& 21 \Rightarrow 2 \times 19=38 \text { numbers } \\ & 19 \rightarrow 1 \& 37,2 \& 36,3 \& 35, \cdots 16 \& 22,17 \& 21, \\ & 18 \& 20 \Rightarrow 2 \times 18=36 \text { numbers } \end{aligned} $$ $\therefore 20$ has the greatest number of distinct rhyming prime around it. ",dataset/time_series_split/train.json#761
mathedu,mathedu:2a9174863a7589f1,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Treats squaring as multiplication by 2,ok,2(a+b)-\frac{2(a-b)}{a b},ok,the value of ( 2.502 + 0.064 ) ^ 2 - ( 2.502 - 0.064 ) ^ 2 / 2.502 * 0.064,"substitute $a$ for 2.502 , and $b$ for 0.064 in the formula. $$ \begin{aligned} \Rightarrow & 2(a+b)-\frac{2(a-b)}{a b} \\ & =2 a+2 b-\frac{2 a}{a b}+\frac{2 b}{a b} \end{aligned} $$",dataset/time_series_split/train.json#762
mathedu,mathedu:edcc306f0ff85225,,Careless error,Uses the wrong fraction in the difference equation,ok,x-y=\frac{1}{2}(x+y),ok,"right now , al and eliot have bank accounts , and al has more money than eliot . the difference between their two accounts is 1 / 12 of the sum of their two accounts . if al ’ s account were to increase by 10 % and eliot ’ s account were to increase by 20 % , then al would have exactly $ 20 more than eliot in his account . how much money does eliot have in his account right now ?","Assume al have $x$ and eliot have $y$ $$ \begin{aligned} & x-y=\frac{1}{2}(x+y) \\ & 1.1 x-1.2 y=20 \\ & \frac{1}{2} x=\frac{3}{2} y \\ & x=3 y \\ & 3.3 y-1.2 y=20 \\ & 2.1 y=20 , \quad y=\frac{200}{21} \end{aligned} $$",dataset/time_series_split/train.json#772
