mathedu,mathedu:018f1e0622a4a197,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,"Uses an incorrect sphere-volume formula, cubing the diameter instead of the radius",ok,\text { sphere volume }=\frac{3}{4} \pi \cdot 6^3=162 \pi\left(\mathrm{cm}^3\right),ok,the measurement of a rectangular box with lid is 25 cmx 6 cmx 18 cm . find the volume of the largest sphere that can be inscribed in the box ( in terms of π cm 3 ) . ( hint : the lowest measure of rectangular box represents the diameter of the largest sphere ),\text { sphere volume }=\frac{3}{4} \pi \cdot 6^3=162 \pi\left(\mathrm{cm}^3\right),dataset/time_series_split/train.json#784
mathedu,mathedu:7a36c945c3a36305,,Measurement error,Fails to convert speed units before dividing distance by speed,ok,times they need is $\frac{380}{115}=\frac{76}{23}$,ok,"two trains each 190 m in length each , are running on two parallel lines in opposite directions . if one goes at the speed of 65 km / h while the other travels at 50 km / h . how long will it take for them to pass each other completely .",$$ 65+50=115 $$ distance to pass is $190 \times 2=380$ times they need is $\frac{380}{115}=\frac{76}{23}$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#791
mathedu,mathedu:a490495fc5055817,,Unfinished answer,,label_conflict,& x=250(rs),ok,"a women purchased 3 towels @ rs . 100 each , 5 towels @ rs . 150 each and two towels at a certain rate which is now slipped off from his memory . but she remembers that the average price of the towels was rs . 155 . find the unknown rate of two towels ?",assumes the unknown towel price $X$ $$ \begin{aligned} & \frac{3 \times 100+5 \times 150+2 \times x}{10}=155 \\ & 1050+2 x=1550 \\ & x=250(rs) \end{aligned} $$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#793
mathedu,mathedu:f2aa91b2721ddce0,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Uses a union formula instead of combining matching parity cases,ok,\frac{2}{5}+\frac{2}{4}-\frac{2}{5} \times \frac{2}{4},ok,"if x is to be chosen at random from the integers between 1 to 5 , inclusive , and y is to be chosen at random from the integers between 7 and 10 , inclusive , what is the probability that x + y will be even ?",\begin{aligned} & \frac{2}{5}+\frac{2}{4}-\frac{2}{5} \times \frac{2}{4} \\ = & \frac{8+10-4}{20} \\ = & \frac{4}{5} \end{aligned},dataset/time_series_split/train.json#798
mathedu,mathedu:6acf6fb779d7b925,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Averages speeds using distance weights instead of dividing total distance by total time,ok,24 \times \frac{12}{60}+48 \times \frac{48}{60},ok,"tom traveled the entire 60 miles trip . if he did the first 12 miles of at a constant rate 24 miles per hour and the remaining trip of at a constant rate 48 miles per hour , what is the his average speed , in miles per hour ?",\begin{aligned} & 24 \times \frac{12}{60}+48 \times \frac{48}{60} \\ & =4.8+37.6=32.4 \text { mile } / \text { hour } \end{aligned},dataset/time_series_split/train.json#800
mathedu,mathedu:8f0d010c560bd7bc,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Treats added impurities as reducing the quantity instead of increasing it,ok,80 \times(1-35 \%),ok,a dishonest dealer claims to sell a product at its cost price . he uses a counterfeit weight which is 20 % less than the real weight . further greed overtook him and he added 35 % impurities to the product . find the net profit percentage of the dealer ?,Assume the cost price of $100 \mathrm{~kg}$ product is $\$ 100$. $$ \begin{aligned} & 100 \times(1-20 \%)=80 \mathrm{~kg} \\ & 80 \times(1-35 \%) \\ & =80 \times 0.65 \end{aligned} $$ $=52 \mathrm{~kg} \Rightarrow$ real weight of product 52 \mathrm{~kg}$ product $\Rightarrow$ cost price is $\$ 52$ $100-52=48 \Rightarrow$ profit $$ \frac{48}{52}=\frac{12}{13} = 0.923=92.3 \% $$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#811
