mathedu,mathedu:635b38885250cf11,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Uses an incorrect sum of lengths as the crossing distance,ok,\frac{200}{\frac{8}{3.6}},ok,"two trains 200 m and 150 m long are running on parallel rails at the rate of 40 kmph and 48 kmph respectively . in how much time will they cross each other , if they are running in the same direction ?",$$ 150+(200+50) $$ $=200 \Rightarrow$ the second train need to chace $$ \begin{aligned} & 48-40=8 \mathrm{~km} / \mathrm{hr}=\frac{8}{3.6} \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s} \\ & \begin{aligned} \frac{200}{\frac{8}{3.6}} & =200 \times \frac{3.6}{8} \\ & =90(\mathrm{~s}) \end{aligned} \end{aligned} $$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#926
mathedu,mathedu:fd912df9512aa609,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Subtracts fractions with unlike denominators incorrectly,ok,\frac{\left(\frac{13}{40}-\frac{1}{4}\right)}{\frac{1}{4}} \ =\frac{1}{4},ok,"a certain car uses one gallon of gasoline every 32 miles when it travels on highway , and one gallon of gasoline every 20 miles when it travels in the city . when a car travels 4 miles on highway and 4 additional miles in the city , it uses what percent more gasoline than if it travels 8 miles on the highway ?",city $=\frac{1}{20}$ gallon/mile highway: $\frac{1}{32}$ gallon/mile $$ \begin{aligned} & 4 \times \frac{1}{20}+4 \times \frac{1}{32} \\ & =\frac{1}{5}+\frac{1}{8} \\ & \begin{array}{l} =\frac{13}{40} \text { gallon }   8 \times \frac{1}{32} \end{array} \\ &  \text { gallon }=\frac{\frac{1}{40}}{\frac{1}{4}}  \\ & \end{aligned} $$ \frac{\left(\frac{13}{40}-\frac{1}{4}\right)}{\frac{1}{4}} \\ =\frac{1}{4}  \\ =10 \%,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#930
