mathedu,mathedu:feaa4824d778b61a,,Algebraic error,Makes an arithmetic subtraction error when solving for the principal,ok,x+3 \times 24=830 \quad \& x=808,ok,peter invests a sum of money and gets back an amount of $ 830 in 3 years . david invests an equal amount of money and gets an amount of $ 854 in 4 years . if both amounts were invested at the same rate ( simple interest ) what was the sum of money invested ?,"assume the amount of money $x$, and the interest rate i $$ \begin{aligned} & \left\{\begin{array}{l} x \times(1+i \times 3)=830 \\ x \times(1+i \times 4)=854 \end{array}\right. \\ & \left\{\begin{array}{l} x+3 i x=830 -(1)\\ x+4 i x=854 -(2)\end{array}\right. \\ & (2)-(1) i x=24 \\ & x+3 \times 24=830 \\ & x=808 \end{aligned} $$",dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1061
mathedu,mathedu:4ef12c802ef30af0,,Comprehension error,Mishandles the exponent when rewriting a power of 4 as a power of 2,ok,7-6=1,ok,"in the game of dubblefud , red chips , blue chips and green chips are each worth 2 , 4 and 5 points respectively . in a certain selection of chips , the product of the point values of the chips is 16000 . if the number of blue chips in this selection doubles the number of green chips , how many red chips are in the selection ?",Assume we have $x$ green chips y red chips $$ \begin{aligned} & 5^x \times 4^{2 x} \times 2^y=16000 \\ & 16000=2^7 \times 5^3 \\ & x=3 \\ & 7-6=1 \\ & y=1 \end{aligned} $$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1071
mathedu,mathedu:bb9b8a2513f11b3a,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Uses distance–speed products instead of total distance,ok,\frac{99+99}{\frac{9}{11}+\frac{11}{9}},ok,a cyclist rides a bicycle 9 km at an average speed of 11 km / hr and again travels 11 km at an average speed of 9 km / hr . what is the average speed for the entire trip ?,\begin{aligned} & \frac{99+99}{\frac{9}{11}+\frac{11}{9}} \\ = & \frac{198}{\frac{81+121}{99}} \\ = & 198 \times \frac{99}{202} \\ =  & 97.04 \mathrm{~km} / \mathrm{hr} \end{aligned},dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1076
mathedu,mathedu:48590ffab905f58d,,Lack of necessary mathematical concepts,Combines mutually exclusive payment scenarios into one total-pay equation,ok,(10 x+10 x \times 15) \times t+10 x \times 10=300,ok,"p and q are the only two applicants qualified for a short - term research project that pays 300 dollars in total . candidate p has more experience and , if hired , would be paid 50 percent more per hour than candidate q would be paid . candidate q , if hired , would require 10 hours more than candidate p to do the job . candidate p ’ s hourly wage is how many dollars greater than candidate q ’ s hourly wage ?",Assume $q$ would be paid $x$ per hour $$ \begin{aligned} & (10 x+10 x \times 15) \times t+10 x \times 10=300 \\ & 25 x \times t+100 x=300 \\ & (100+25 t) x=300 \end{aligned} $$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1079
