mathedu,mathedu:5f581ae259bea756,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Uses the longer length instead of the sum of both lengths,ok,"(200-150) =50
50+150 =200 m",ok,"two trains 200 m and 150 m long are running on parallel rails at the rate of 40 kmph and 45 kmph respectively . in how much time will they cross each other , if they are running in the same direction ?",$$ \begin{aligned} (200-150) & =50 \\ 50+150 & =200 \mathrm{~m} \end{aligned} $$ $\Rightarrow$ total distance $$ \begin{aligned} 45 \mathrm{~km}-40 \mathrm{~km}=5 \mathrm{~km} & \\ 5 \mathrm{~km} / \mathrm{hr}=5000 \div 60 \div 60 & =\frac{50}{36} \\ & =\frac{25}{18} \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{sec} \end{aligned} $$ $\Rightarrow$ the gap between the two train per second. $$ 200 \div \frac{25}{18}=200 \times \frac{18}{25}=8 \times 18=144 $$ seconds,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1290
