mathedu,mathedu:2106ce79b286def6,,Comprehension error,Omits the specified percentage when forming the final ratio,ok,m \div 200 \% j=1750 \%,ok,"if 125 % of j is equal to 25 % of k , 150 % of k is equal to 50 % of l , and 175 % of l is equal to 75 % of m , then 30 % of m is equal to what percent of 200 % of j ?",\begin{aligned} & 125 \% j=25 \% k \\ & k=500 \% j \\ & 150 \% k=50 \% I \\ & I=1500 \% j \\ & 175 \% I=75 \% m \\ & m=3500 \% j \\ & m \div 200 \% j=1750 \% \end{aligned},dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1308
mathedu,mathedu:7ba21a0840250c47,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Confuses the number of operating cycles with elapsed minutes,ok,\frac{60 r}{3 r+2 r-4 r}=60  & 60-1=59 \text { (mins) },ok,"three pipes , a , b , & c are attached to a tank . a & b can fill it in 20 & 30 minutes respectively while c can empty it in 15 minutes . if a , b & c are kept open successively for 1 minute each , how soon will the tank be filled ?","$$ [20,30,15]=2^2 \times 3 \times 5=60 $$ assume the capacity of the tank is 60r. $$ \left\{\begin{array}{l} a^{\prime} \text { 's injection: bor } \div 20=3 r(\text { per minute }) \\ b^{\prime} \text { s injection: } 60 r \div 30=2 r(\text { per minute }) \\ \left.c^{\prime} \text { 's displacement : } 60 r \div 15=4 r \text { (per minute }\right) \end{array}\right. $$  \begin{aligned} & \frac{60 r}{3 r+2 r-4 r}=\frac{60 r}{r}=60  & 60-1=59 \text { (mins) } \end{aligned}",dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1313
mathedu,mathedu:de8f9633bf55fc17,,Careless error,Miscounts standard deviations when locating a score relative to the mean,ok,mean score }=60+x=100-3 x,ok,"for a certain exam , a score of 60 was 2 standard deviations below mean and a score of 100 was 3 standard deviations above mean . what was the mean score for the exam ?",assume the standard deviation is $\chi$. $$ \begin{aligned} & \text { mean score }=60+x=100-3 x \\ & \Rightarrow 4 x=40 \\ & x=10 \\ & \end{aligned} $$ $\therefore$ mean score $=60+10=70$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1325
mathedu,mathedu:bb9e3957f29a3738,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Adds the half-and-extra amounts directly instead of accounting for sequential halving,ok,1.5+2.5+3.5+1 =8.5 \text { pennies },ok,a charitable woman gave one penny more than half pennies in her purse to a farmer . afterwards she came across a beggar whom she gave 2 pennies more than half of what she had in her purse . in the end she gave 3 pennies more than half of what she had in her purse and left with a penny with her to a boy . find what she had in the beginning ?,\begin{aligned} & 1.5+2.5+3.5+1 \\ & =8.5 \text { pennies } \end{aligned},dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1326
mathedu,mathedu:63b432e5fff86df0,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Treats quantities in a one-to-two ratio as equal,ok,favorable → e & favorable → b & $x \%$ ... unfavorable → e & favorable → b & $x \%$,ok,"a polling company surveyed a certain country , and it found that 35 % of that country ’ s registered voters had an unfavorable impression of both of that state ’ s major political parties and that 20 % had a favorable impression only of party e . if one registered voter has a favorable impression of both parties for every two registered voters who have a favorable impression only of party b , then what percentage of the country ’ s registered voters have a favorable impression of both parties ( assuming that respondents to the poll were given a choice between favorable and unfavorable impressions only ) ?",assume voters have a favorable impression on party b take $2 x \%$. \begin{tabular}{l|c|c}  & favorable $\rightarrow b$ & unfavorable $\rightarrow b$ \\ \hline favorable $\rightarrow e$ & $x \%$ & $20 \%$ \\ \hline unfavorable $\rightarrow e$ & $x \%$ & $35 \%$ \\ \hline total & $2 x \%$ & $55 \%$ \\ $2 x+55=100$ \\ $2 x=45$ \\ $x=27.5$ \end{tabular} $\therefore$ voters have a favorable impression of both parties take $27.5 \%$.,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1329
mathedu,mathedu:95c3fe08e1a3e66d,,Careless error,Uses John's speed instead of Sam's speed to calculate Sam's time,ok,Sam has to spend $\frac{100}{6} = 16.66$ sec to finish $100 \mathrm{~m}$,ok,"in a 100 m race , sam beats john by 4 seconds . on the contrary , if sam allowed john to start 24 m ahead of sam , then sam and john reach the finishing point at the same time . how long does sam take to run the 100 m race ?","assume the speed of $\operatorname{John} X$, Sam $Y(\mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{s})$ $$ \begin{aligned} & \left\{\begin{array}{l} \frac{100}{x}=\frac{100}{y}+4 \\ \frac{100-24}{x}=\frac{100}{y} \end{array}\right. \\ & \begin{cases}100 y=100 x+4 x y & 100 \frac{25}{19} x=100 x+4 \cdot \frac{25}{19} x^2 \\ 76 y=100 x & 2500 x=1900 x+100 x^2 \end{cases} \end{aligned} \\ y=\frac{25}{19} x & x^2-6 x=0 \\ & x(x-6)=0 \\ x=6,0 \text { (0 impossible) }$$ Sam has to spend $\frac{100}{6} = 16.66$ sec to finish $100 \mathrm{~m}$",dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1330
