mathedu,mathedu:10c8b3a81f71dc87,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Treats a percentage deficit as a corresponding increase,ok,(1.14 x \cdot 1.05 y)-x y,ok,"in measuring the sides of a rectangle , one side is taken 14 % in excess and other 5 % in deficit . find the error percentage in the area calculated from these measurements .",\begin{aligned} & (1.14 x \cdot 1.05 y)-x y  \\ & =1.197 x y-x y \\ & =0.197 x y \end{aligned}  \\ \frac{0.197 x y}{x y}=0.197=19.7 \%,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1533
mathedu,mathedu:f4010c992f28ecdf,,Lack of necessary mathematical concepts,Adds payments from mutually exclusive hiring scenarios together,ok,x \times(t+10)+x \times(1+50 \%) \times t=360,ok,"p and q are the only two applicants qualified for a short - term research project that pays 360 dollars in total . candidate p has more experience and , if hired , would be paid 50 percent more per hour than candidate q would be paid . candidate q , if hired , would require 10 hours more than candidate p to do the job . candidate p ’ s hourly wage is how many dollars greater than candidate q ’ s hourly wage ?",Assume $q$ would be paid $x$ per hour $x \times(t+10)+x \times(1+50 \%) \times t=360$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1534
mathedu,mathedu:12100c08a25abd9d,,Unfinished answer,Stops after finding an intermediate value without calculating the requested difference.,ok,x =20,ok,"p and q are the only two applicants qualified for a short - term research project that pays 420 dollars in total . candidate p has more experience and , if hired , would be paid 50 percent more per hour than candidate q would be paid . candidate q , if hired , would require 10 hours more than candidate p to do the job . candidate p ’ s hourly wage is how many dollars greater than candidate q ’ s hourly wage ?",\begin{aligned} &\begin{aligned} & \frac{420}{x}=\frac{420}{(x+10)} \times 1.5 \\ & \frac{420}{x}=\frac{630}{x+10} \end{aligned}\\ &\begin{aligned} 420 x+4200 & =630 x \\ 4200 & =210 x \\ x & =20 \end{aligned} \end{aligned},dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1537
mathedu,mathedu:2049e89241747d4b,,Comprehension error,Applies the reduction percentages to the wrong parts of the fraction.,ok,2 \times \frac{y}{x}=\frac{\frac{1}{5} y^2}{\frac{2}{3} x^2},ok,"a fraction in reduced form is such that when it is squared and then its numerator is reduced by 33 ( 1 / 3 ) % and denominator is reduced to 20 % , its result is twice the original fraction . the sum of numerator and denominator is :",\begin{aligned} & \left(\frac{y}{x}\right)^2=\frac{y^2}{x^2} \\ & 2 \times \frac{y}{x}=\frac{\frac{1}{5} y^2}{\frac{2}{3} x^2} \\ & 2=\frac{3}{10} \times \frac{y}{x} \\ & \frac{y}{x}=\frac{20}{3} \\ & 3+20=23 \end{aligned},dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1545
mathedu,mathedu:3d43527d4caa8e01,,Comprehension error,Fails to account for the ten-year decrease in the past age,ok,(a+10)=2 b,ok,"in 10 years , a will be twice as old as b was 10 years ago . if a is now 9 years older than b , the present age of b is ?",\begin{aligned} & a=b+9 \\ & (a+10)=2 b \\ & \text { put }(a=b+9) \text { into }(a+10)=2 b \\ & \Rightarrow(b+9)+10=2 b \\ & \Rightarrow b+19=2 b \\ & \Rightarrow b=19 \end{aligned},dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1547
