mathedu,mathedu:2dddedeb96e24997,,Unfinished answer,Reports the base speed instead of the requested doubled speed,ok,Assume speed is $x \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{sec}$ ... $x=15$,ok,"angelina walked 840 meters from her home to the grocery at a constant speed . she then walked 480 meters to the gym at double the speed . she spent 40 seconds less on her way from the grocery to the gym than on her way from home to the grocery . what was angelina ' s speed , in meters per second , from the grocery to the gym ?",Assume speed is $x \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{sec}$ $$ \begin{aligned} & \frac{840}{x}-40=\frac{480}{2 x} \\ & 40 x=600 \\ & x=15 \end{aligned} $$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1566
mathedu,mathedu:809eb0cadb9fded2,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Uses the selling price instead of the cost price as the gain denominator,ok,\text { gain percent }= \frac{0.5 x-0.25 x}{0.5 x},ok,the cost price of an article is 25 % of the marked price . calculate the gain percent after allowing a discount of 50 % .,assume the original price $X$ $$ \cos t=0.25 x $$ $50 \%$ discount $\Rightarrow$ selling price $0.5 x$ $$ \begin{aligned} \text { gain percent }= & \frac{0.5 x-0.25 x}{0.5 x} \\ & =50 \% \end{aligned} $$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#1569
