mathedu,mathedu:b6c1b7a52736fdfc,,Careless error,Counts nine possibilities for the unrestricted digit instead of ten,ok,8 \times 9=72,ok,"how many integers k greater than 100 and less than 1000 are there such that if the hundreds and the unit digits of k are reversed , the resulting integer is k + 99 ?","if $k=a \times 100+b \times 10+c$ then $$ \begin{aligned} & a \times 100+b \times 10+c=c \times 100+b \times 10+a-99 \\ & 99 a-99 c=- 99 \\ & a-c=-1 \\ & a=c-1 \\ & 102,112, \ldots .192 \Rightarrow 9 \\ & 203,213, \ldots .293 \Rightarrow 9 \\ & 809,819, \ldots 899 \Rightarrow 9 \\ & 8 \times 9=72 \end{aligned} $$",dataset/time_series_split/train.json#2059
mathedu,mathedu:081a51f80e6fb26c,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Calculates the probability of success exactly on the fourth draw instead of on or after it,ok,\left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^3 \times \frac{1}{4}=\frac{27}{256},ok,"there are 13 clubs in a full deck of 52 cards . in a certain game , you pick a card from a standard deck of 52 cards . if the card is a club , you win . if the card is not a club , the person replaces the card to the deck , reshuffles , and draws again . the person keeps repeating that process until he picks a club , and the point is to measure how many draws it took before the person picked a club and , thereby , won . what is the probability that one will pick the first club on the forth draw or later ?",the probability of drawing clubs: $\frac{13}{52}=\frac{1}{4}$ the probability of not drawing clubs : $\frac{3}{4}$ $\left(\frac{3}{4}\right)^3 \times \frac{1}{4}=\frac{27}{256}$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#2061
mathedu,mathedu:ffd27a5d1515491d,,Measurement error,Incorrectly converts speeds from kilometers per hour to meters per second,ok,52 km / hr =52000 m / hr=20 m / sec,ok,two trains of equal are running on parallel lines in the same direction at 52 km / hr and 36 km / hr . the faster train passes the slower train in 36 sec . the length of each train is ?,assume the length of each train is $x \mathrm{~m}$. $$ \begin{aligned} 52 \mathrm{~km} / \mathrm{hr} & =52000 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{hr}=20 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{sec} \\ 36 \mathrm{~km} / \mathrm{hr} & =36000 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{hr}=10 \mathrm{~m} / \mathrm{sec} \\ \frac{2 x}{20-10} & =36 \\ 2 x & =360 \\ x & =180(\mathrm{~m}) \end{aligned} $$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#2064
