mathedu,mathedu:d2b54ccdecfa0b1e,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Subtracts the percentage from an increased quantity instead of reversing the increase factor,ok,rate of flow in first hour is $36 \times(1-50 \%)=18$,ok,"the flow of water through a drainage pipe was monitored for a 3 - hour period . in the second hour , the rate of flow was 36 gallons per hour , which was 50 percent faster than the rate of flow for the first hour . if 25 percent more water flowed through the pipe in the third hour than it did in the second , how many gallons of water flowed through the pipe during the entire 3 hours ?",rate of flow in first hour is $36 \times(1-50 \%)=18$ rate of flow in third hour is $36 \times(1+25 \%)=45$ So there are $18+36+45=99$ gallons of water flow through during the entire 3 hours.,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#2494
mathedu,mathedu:f2426b42e68aa4e3,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Uses a difference of speeds instead of a difference of travel times,ok,\frac{48}{\frac{48}{x}-2}-\frac{48}{2 x}=1,ok,"in covering a distance of 48 km , abhay takes 2 hours more than sameer . if abhay doubles his speed , then he would take 1 hour less than sameer . abhay ' s speed is :",assume abhay's speed x km/ hr $\frac{48}{\frac{48}{x}-2} \Rightarrow$ sameer's speed $$ \begin{array}{ll} \frac{48}{\frac{48}{x}-2}-\frac{48}{2 x}=1  \\ \frac{48}{\frac{48}{x}-2}=\left(1+\frac{48}{2x}\right) \\ 48=\frac{48}{x}-2+\frac{48}{2x}\left(\frac{48}{x}-2\right) \\ 48=\frac{48}{x}-2+\frac{48^2}{2 x^2}-\frac{48}{x} \\ 96 x^2=96 x-4 x^2+48^2 -96x \\ 100 x^2 = 2304 \\ x^2 = 34.04 \\  x= \pm \sqrt{23.4} \\ & = \pm 4.8 \\ Ans:4.8 km/hr \end{array} $$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#2502
mathedu,mathedu:e9c3dc4062b43a4b,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Confuses stopping time per travel hour with stopping time per total hour,ok,"every hour the train travels, it stops for \(\frac{1}{4}\) hour, which is 15 mins.",ok,"without stoppages , a train travels certain distance with an average speed of 200 km / h , and with stoppages , it covers the same distance with an average speed of 160 km / h . how many minutes per hour the train stops ?","in the case of without stoppage, assume the train travels $x \mathrm{~km}$ and takes $y$ hours in total. then assume the train stops $z$ hours in total. $$ \left\{\begin{array} { l }  { \frac { x } { y } = 2 0 0 } \\ { \frac { x } { y + z } = 1 6 0 } \end{array} \Rightarrow \left\{\begin{array}{l} x=200 y \ldots \text { (1) } \\ x=160 y+160 z \ldots \text { (2) } \end{array}\right.\right. $$ substite $200 y$ for $x$ in (2) $$ \begin{aligned} \Rightarrow 200 y & =160 y+160 z \\ 40 y & =160 z \\ y & =4 z \Rightarrow y: z=4: 1=1: \frac{1}{4} \end{aligned} $$ $\therefore$ every hour the train travels, it stops for $\frac{1}{4}$ hour, which is 15 mins.",dataset/time_series_split/train.json#2507
mathedu,mathedu:3f9e77b9050b4117,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Uses a simple-interest expression instead of annual compound growth,ok,4410 & =2 x(1+5 \%),ok,a sum amounts to rs . 4410 in 2 years at the rate of 5 % p . a . if interest was compounded yearly then what was the principal ?,assume the principal is $x$. $$ \begin{aligned} 4410 & =2 x(1+5 \%) \\ 4410 & =2.1 x \\ x & =2100 \end{aligned} $$,dataset/time_series_split/train.json#2508
