mathedu,mathedu:1203420847ca26ca,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Forms the profit-sharing ratio from incorrect capital amounts,ok,\frac{2000}{2000+500} \times 798,ok,"a and b began business with rs . 3000 and rs . 4000 after 8 months , a withdraws rs . 1000 and b advances rs . 1000 more . at the end of the year , their profits amounted to rs . 798 find the share of a .",\begin{aligned} \text { a's share } & =\frac{2000}{2000+500} \times 798 \\ & =\frac{2}{7} \times 798 \\ & =228\left(rs \right) \end{aligned},dataset/time_series_split/test.json#216
mathedu,mathedu:a58f83f5ad819570,,Arithmetical error,Makes an arithmetic error when simplifying a fractional work rate,ok,=\frac{1}{15}+\frac{1}{14}=\frac{29}{210},ok,25 women can complete a work in 7 days and 10 children take 14 days to complete the work . how many days will 5 women and 10 children take to complete the work ?,$$ \begin{aligned} & \frac{1}{25 \times 7}=\frac{1}{175} \text { per woman per day } \\ & \frac{1}{10 \times 14}=\frac{1}{140} \text { per child per day } \end{aligned} $$ $$ \begin{aligned} & 5 \times \frac{1}{175}+10 \times \frac{1}{140} \\ & =\frac{1}{15}+\frac{1}{14}=\frac{29}{210} \\ & \frac{1}{\frac{29}{210}} = 7.2 \end{aligned} $$ Ans 8 days,dataset/time_series_split/test.json#217 dataset/time_series_split/test.json#301
mathedu,mathedu:668a790f3e576bd5,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Counts successive choices independently instead of enforcing all required inequalities.,ok,7 \times 7+6 \times 6+5 \times 5+4 \times 4+3 \times 3+2 \times 2,ok,a bag contains 10 balls numbered from 0 to 9 . the balls are such that the person picking a ball out of the bag is equally likely to pick anyone of them . a person picked a ball and replaced it in the bag after noting its number . he repeated this process 2 more times . what is the probability that the ball picked first is numbered higher than the ball picked second and the ball picked second is numbered higher than the ball picked third ?,"\begin{aligned} & \text { if } z=1, y=2 \sim 8, z=3 \sim 9 \\ & z=2, y=3 \sim 8, z=4 \sim 9 \\ & \vdots \\ & z=7, y=8, z=9 \\ & 7 \times 7+6 \times 6+5 \times 5+4 \times 4+3 \times 3+2 \times 2 \\ & +1 \\ & =49+36+25+16+9+4+1=140 \\ & \frac{140}{10 \times 10 \times 10}=14 \% \end{aligned}",dataset/time_series_split/test.json#221
mathedu,mathedu:ac9a30045fe57b76,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Uses the probability of all trials succeeding for every success count,ok,once out of 4 time: \(\frac{1}{2}\)^4=\frac{1}{16}\n twice out of 4 time: \(\frac{1}{2}\)^4=\frac{1}{16},ok,"on a ranch , a rancher can place a loop of rope , called a lasso , once in every 2 throws around a cow ’ s neck . what is the probability that the rancher will be able to place a lasso around a cow ’ s neck at least once in 4 attempts ?",the probability that the rancher success in placing a lasso $=\frac{1}{2}$ $$ \text { the probability of success }\left\{\begin{array}{l} \text { once out of } 4 \text { time: }\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^4=\frac{1}{16} \\ \text { twice out of } 4 \text { time: }\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^4=\frac{1}{16} \\ \text { three times out of } 4 \text { time: }\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^4=\frac{1}{16} \\ \text { four times out of } 4 \text { time : }\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)^4=\frac{1}{16} \end{array}\right. \\ \Rightarrow  \frac{1}{16} \times 4 = \frac{1}{4}$$,dataset/time_series_split/test.json#226
