mathedu,mathedu:94599b73bf59a302,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Confuses the number of qualifying sets with the number of multiples counted.,ok,the sets contain (96-1)=95 multiples of 6,ok,"consider the sets tn = { n , n + 1 , n + 2 , n + 3 , n + 4 ) , where n = 1 , 2 , 3 , … , 96 . how many of these sets contain 6 or any integral multiple thereof ( i . e . , any one of the numbers 6 , 12 , 18 , … ) ?","\begin{aligned} & t_1=1,2,3,4,5 \text { (there are no multiples of } 6 \text { ) } \\ & t_2=2,3,4,5,6 \\ & t_3=3,4,5,6,7 \\ & \vdots \\ & t_10=10,11,12,13,14 \\ & t_11=11,12,13,14,15 \\ & \vdots \\ & t_96=96,97,98,99,100 \\ & \Rightarrow \text { when } n=1,2,3, \ldots, 96 \text {, the sets contain }(96-1)=95 \text { multiples of } 6 . \end{aligned}",dataset/time_series_split/test.json#244
