mathedu,mathedu:69ecbbd0fa0c430a,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Confuses the probability that neither event occurs with at least one occurring,ok,(1-x)\left(1-\frac{1}{2} x\right) =3 x \cdot \frac{1}{2} x,ok,"the events a and b are independent , the probability that event a occurs is greater than 0 , and the probability that event a occurs is twice the probability that event b occurs . the probability that at least one of events a and b occurs is 3 times the probability that both events a and b occur . what is the probability that event a occurs ?",assume the probability that event a occurs is $x$. probability of happening $\left\{\begin{array}{l}\text { event } a: x \\ \text { event } b: \frac{1}{2} x\end{array}\right.$ $$ \begin{aligned} (1-x)\left(1-\frac{1}{2} x\right) & =3 x \cdot \frac{1}{2} x \\ \frac{1}{2} x^2-\frac{3}{2} x+1 & =\frac{3}{2} x^2 \\ x^2+\frac{3}{2} x-1 & =0 \\ \left(x-\frac{1}{2}\right)(x+2) & =0 \\ x=\frac{1}{2} \text { or } x & =-2 \end{aligned} $$ $\Rightarrow \because$ the probability of happening event $a$ is greater than 0 . $$ \therefore x=\frac{1}{2}=50 \%  $$,dataset/time_series_split/test.json#352
