mathedu,mathedu:c17371985136402a,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Divides the remaining total by the original number of students,ok,\frac{80 x-5 \times 40}{x} =90,ok,"the average mark of the students of a class in a particular exam is 80 . if 5 students whose average mark in that exam is 40 are excluded , the average mark of the remaining will be 90 . find the number of students who wrote the exam ?",assume $\chi$ students wrote the exam. $$ \begin{aligned} \frac{80 x-5 \times 40}{x} & =90 \\ 80 x-200 & =90 x \\ 10 x & =200 \quad x=20 \end{aligned} $$,dataset/time_series_split/test.json#568
mathedu,mathedu:442d28a9c72a0cae,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Calculates profit percentage using selling price instead of cost price,ok,\frac{t \times 0.9-90}{t \times 0.9}=20 \%,ok,"a retailer bought a machine at a wholesale price of $ 90 and later on sold it after a 10 % discount of the retail price . if the retailer made a profit equivalent to 20 % of the whole price , what is the retail price t of the machine ?",\begin{aligned} & \frac{t \times 0.9-90}{t \times 0.9}=20 \% \\ & 0.9 t-90=0.18 t \\ & 0.72 t=90 \\ & t=90 \times \frac{100}{72} \\ & =125 \text { (dollars) } \end{aligned},dataset/time_series_split/test.json#570
mathedu,mathedu:d8faf010802b8f55,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Multiplies the speed difference by the time difference to find distance,ok,(40-30) \times \frac{1}{5}=2(\mathrm{~km}),ok,"john goes to his office by car at a speed of 40 kmph and reaches 8 minutes earlier . if he goes at a speed of 30 kmph , he reaches 4 mins late . what is the distance from his house to office ?",assume the distance from his house to office is $x(\mathrm{~km})$. $$ \begin{aligned} & 12 \text { mins }=\frac{1}{5} \text { hrs } \\ & (40-30) \times \frac{1}{5}=2(\mathrm{~km})  \end{aligned} $$,dataset/time_series_split/test.json#572
mathedu,mathedu:ac38d4f2e3529f8a,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Counts unordered outcomes while using an ordered sample space,ok,\frac{3+1}{6 \times 6}=\frac{1}{9},ok,"when two dice are rolled , what is the probability that the sum is either 7 or 11 ?","\begin{aligned} & \text { sum }=7: 1+6,2+5,3+4 \\ & \text { sum }=11: 5+6 \\ & \frac{3+1}{6 \times 6}=\frac{1}{9} \end{aligned}",dataset/time_series_split/test.json#575
mathedu,mathedu:bfa78ca8ea2ca243,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Uses the ratio of values instead of their relative difference for percentage error,ok,n \div 9 \div(n \times 9)=\frac{1}{81} = 1 \%,ok,"instead of multiplying a number by 9 , the number is divided by 9 . what is the percentage of error obtained ?",n \div 9 \div(n \times 9)=\frac{1}{81} = 1 \%,dataset/time_series_split/test.json#576
mathedu,mathedu:83754dbe68ffe103,,Measurement error,,label_conflict,\frac{35 \times 1.25}{40-35}=\frac{43.75}{5}=8.75(\mathrm{hr}),ok,"car x began traveling at an average speed of 35 miles per hour . after 72 minutes , car y began traveling at an average speed of 40 miles per hour . when both cars had traveled the same distance , both cars stopped . how many miles did car x travel from the time car y began traveling until both cars stopped ?",assume both car stopped after traveling $r$ miles. $$ \begin{aligned} & 72 \text { mins }=1.25 \mathrm{hrs} \\ & \frac{35 \times 1.25}{40-35}=\frac{43.75}{5}=8.75(\mathrm{hr}) \\ & 35 \times 8.75=306.25 \text { (miles)  } \end{aligned} $$,dataset/time_series_split/test.json#596
mathedu,mathedu:9890662f98c2d6e7,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Uses the wrong difference between the two lateness values,ok,=35-10 \  & t=\frac{32}{25},ok,"a train after traveling for 50 km meets with an accident and then proceeds at 3 / 4 of its former speed and arrives at its destination 35 minutes late . had the accident occurred 24 km farther , it would have reached the destination only 25 minutes late . what is the speed t of the train .",Assume the destination is $X \mathrm{~km}$ far away $$ \begin{aligned} & ((x-50-24)-(x-50)) \div \frac{3}{4} t=35-10 \\ & t=\frac{32}{25} \mathrm{~km} / \mathrm{min} \end{aligned} $$,dataset/time_series_split/test.json#597
mathedu,mathedu:b458e81fbf792251,,Wrong mathematical operation/concept,Adds probabilities with different conditioning events as though they shared the same condition,ok,\left(1-\frac{1}{10}\right) \times\left(\frac{1}{9}+\frac{1}{8}\right)=\frac{17}{80},ok,"a certain club has 10 members , including parry . one of the 10 members is chosen at random to be the president , one of the remaining 9 members is to be chosen at random to be the secretary , and one of the remaining 8 members is to be chosen at random to be the treasurer . what is the probability that parry will be either the member chose to be secretary or the member chose to be treasurer ?",\left(1-\frac{1}{10}\right) \times\left(\frac{1}{9}+\frac{1}{8}\right)=\frac{17}{80},dataset/time_series_split/test.json#599
stepwise,stepwise:b5399468ec42c098,,Missing / Wrong factual knowledge,Multiplies the number of bicycles rather than the total number of tires,ok,his friend must have 3 x 4 = 12 bicycle tires,ok,"Ignatius owns 4 bicycles.  A friend of his owns different types of cycles, which have three times are many tires as Ignatius's bikes have. He has one unicycle, a tricycle, and the rest are bikes. How many bicycles does the friend own?","Since Ignatius owns 4 bicycles, and his friend has more tires, then his friend must have 3 x 4 = 12 bicycle tires.
The unicycle has 1 tire, and the tricycle has 3 tires, so the total number of tires accounted for is 1 + 3 = 4.
Therefore, the friend must have a total of 12 - 4 = 8 bicycle tires from the remaining cycles.
Since each bike has 2 tires, then the friend must have 8/2 = 4 bicycles.
 4",dataset/dataset.json#0 dataset/dataset.json#181 dataset/dataset.json#768
stepwise,stepwise:96cc2fb8da510e42,,Calculation error easily solved by a calculator,Divides by total ratio parts instead of the corresponding ratio part,ok,45/(5+3) = 5.625 sets of 5 red and 3 blue candles,ok,"On her birthday, Avianna bought red candles and blue candles in the ratio of 5:3. If Avianna had 45 red candles on her birthday, how many blue candles did she have?","The ratio of red candles to blue candles is 5:3, which means for every 5 red candles, there are 3 blue candles.
If Avianna had 45 red candles, then she had 45/(5+3) = 5.625 sets of 5 red and 3 blue candles.
To find the total number of blue candles, we multiply the number of sets by the number of blue candles per set, which is 3.
So, Avianna had 5.625 x 3 = 16.875 blue candles, which we can round to 17.
 17",dataset/dataset.json#1
stepwise,stepwise:0f06c1c8949eb7c9,,Missing / Wrong factual knowledge,,label_conflict,"Therefore, the hill is 1200m high.",ok,"The base of a hill located beside a river is 300m above the seabed. If this depth is a quarter of the vertical distance between the riverbed and the peak of the hill, how high is the hill?","Let's start by setting up an equation to represent the relationship between the different heights of the hill:
height of hill = 4 x depth of river
We know that the depth of the river (which is the base of the hill) is 300m, so we can substitute that value into the equation:
height of hill = 4 x 300
Simplifying the right side of the equation:
height of hill = 1200
Therefore, the hill is 1200m high. 
 1200",dataset/dataset.json#2
stepwise,stepwise:d4a5ccf5f7b804ba,,Misunderstanding of a question,Treats profit as total money and subtracts sales revenue from it,ok,"We know that his total profit for the day was $105, so he must have started with $105 - $54 = $51 worth of watermelons.",ok,Carl has been selling watermelons on the side of the road for $3 each. This evening he went home with $105 in profit and 18 watermelons. How many watermelons did he start out with this morning?,"Let's start by finding out how much money Carl made selling the 18 watermelons. Since he sold each watermelon for $3, he made 18 x $3 = $54.
We know that his total profit for the day was $105, so he must have started with $105 - $54 = $51 worth of watermelons.
Since each watermelon costs $3, he must have started with 51 / $3 = 17 watermelons.
 17",dataset/dataset.json#3
stepwise,stepwise:9bc0075033dba238,,Missing / Wrong factual knowledge,Uses the total number of days instead of subtracting excluded days,ok,John walked the dog for 30 days in April (since there are 4 Sundays he didn't walk the dog).,ok,"John's neighbor tells him to walk his dog for 1 hour each day for a total of $10. He does this for April, save for the 4 Sundays in April. He later spent $50 on books and gave his sister Kaylee the same amount. How much money did John have left?","John walked the dog for 30 days in April (since there are 4 Sundays he didn't walk the dog). So he earned a total of 30 x $10 = $300.
