\]
\[
AC^2 = 100 + PC^2 + 10PC
\]

### Step 5: Using the Pythagorean Theorem in \( \triangle ABC \)
Since \( \angle B = 90^\circ \), we have:
\[
AC^2 = AB^2 + BC^2
\]
Substituting the values:
\[
100 + PC^2 + 10PC = 196 + 36 + PC^2 + 6PC
\]
\[
100 + 10PC = 232 + 6PC
\]
\[
4PC = 132
\]
\[
PC = 33
\]

### Step 6: Increasing \( BC \) by 50%
The new length of \( BC \) is:
\[
BC_{\text{new}} = BC + 0.5 \cdot BC = 2 \cdot BC
\]
\[
BC_{\text{new}} = 2 \cdot \sqrt{36 + 33^2 + 6 \cdot 33} = 2 \cdot \sqrt{36 + 1089 + 198} = 2 \cdot \sqrt{1323} = 2 \cdot 36.37 = 72.74
\]

### Step 7: Finding the New Length of \( PC \)
Using the Law of Cosines again in the new \( \triangle BPC \):
\[
BC_{\text{new}}^2 = PB^2 + PC_{\text{new}}^2 - 2 \cdot PB \cdot PC_{\text{new}} \cdot \cos(120^\circ)
\]
\[
72.74^2 = 36 + PC_{\text{new}}^2 + 6PC_{\text{new}}
\]
\[
5293.00 = 36 + PC_{\text{new}}^2 + 6PC_{\text{new}}
\]
\[
PC_{\text{new}}^2 + 6PC_{\text{new}} - 5257 = 0
\]

Solving this quadratic equation:
\[
PC_{\text{new}} = \frac{-6 \pm \sqrt{6^2 + 4 \cdot 5257}}{2}
\]
\[
PC_{\text{new}} = \frac{-6 \pm \sqrt{36 + 21028}}{2}
\]
\[
PC_{\text{new}} = \frac{-6 \pm \sqrt{21064}}{2}
\]
\[
PC_{\text{new}} = \frac{-6 \pm 145.14}{2}
\]
\[
PC_{\text{new}} = \frac{139.14}{2} = 69.57
\]

Thus, the new length of \( PC \) is approximately:
