eic,eic:8bca82280c753f81,MathQA,adding_irrelevant_information,Adds an unsupported quantity adjustment to the calculation,ok,it is known that one student has no preference and it accounts for one-sixth of one male part.,ok,"The ratio of males to females in a class is 2 : 3. The career preferences of the students in the class are represented in a circle graph. If the area of the graph allocated to each career preference is proportional to the number of students who have that career preference, how many degrees of the circle should be used to represent a career that is preferred by one-half of the males and one-fourth of the females in the class?","There are 2 + 3 = 5 parts in total, but it is known that one student has no preference and it accounts for one-sixth of one male part.
One part represents 360 / 5 = 72 degrees.
One-half of the males is 2 / 2 = 1, less the non-preference portion which is 1/6 of one male part = 1 - 1/6 = 5/6.
One-fourth of the females is 3 / 4 = 0.75.
So, the career that is preferred by one-half of the males (minus the student without preference) and one-fourth of the females should be represented by 5/6 + 0.75 = 1.5833 parts.
Thus, the career should be represented by 1.5833 x 72 ≈ 114 degrees.
#### 114",data/generated_cases_MathQA/adding_irrelevant_information/adding_irrelevant_information_100/generated_cases_clean.jsonl#65
eic,eic:1fc2fd0492e5967d,MathQA,adding_irrelevant_information,Introduces an unsupported assumption that some throwers are also left-handed,ok,"Assuming that among the throwers, 2 are ambidextrous and also count as left-handed players",ok,"Of 70 players on a football team, 31 are throwers. The rest of the team is divided so one third are left-handed and the rest are right-handed. Assuming that all throwers are right-handed, how many right-handed players are there total?","There are 70-31 = 39 non-throwers.
